<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Humphrey IV of Toron</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1166–<a href="/years/1198">1198</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Humphrey IV of Toron was a baron of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a>, stepson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raynald-of-chatillon">Raynald of Châtillon</a> and heir of Toron and Oultrejordain. He married Isabella, half-sister of King Baldwin IV, in 1183, but in 1186 refused to be crowned by the barons opposed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guy-of-lusignan">Guy of Lusignan</a> and submitted to Guy and Sibylla. In 1190 the marriage was annulled so that Isabella could marry <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conrad-of-montferrat">Conrad of Montferrat</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/humphrey-iv-of-toron.jpg" alt="Humphrey IV of Toron" width="320" height="467" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author from northern France · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Humphrey_IV_of_Toron_Isabela.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Humphrey was the son of Humphrey, heir to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humphrey-ii-of-toron">Humphrey II of Toron</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephanie-of-milly">Stephanie of Milly</a>, who inherited the Lordship of Transjordan. His father died around 1173, and his mother married Miles of Plancy and the following year <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raynald-of-chatillon">Raynald of Châtillon</a>. He inherited Toron from his grandfather, who died of wounds from the Battle of Banyas in April 1179. On his betrothal to the eight-year-old Isabella in October 1180 he yielded Toron, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banias">Banias</a> and Chastel Neuf to King Baldwin IV for a money <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> of 7,000 bezants, perhaps so that he would not unite Toron and Transjordan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In September 1183 he led the forces of Oultrejordain against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>'s invasion, and his troops were almost annihilated in an ambush at Mount Gilboa. During his wedding at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-karak">Kerak</a> that autumn Saladin besieged the castle until King Baldwin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raymond-iii-of-tripoli">Raymond III of Tripoli</a> relieved it on 4 December. When the barons gathered at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nablus">Nablus</a> resolved to crown him and Isabella against Sybilla and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guy-of-lusignan">Guy of Lusignan</a>, Humphrey rode secretly to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> by night and swore fealty to Guy, which ended the plot.</p><p>Captured at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hattin">Battle of Hattin</a> on 4 July 1187, he was released when his mother offered the surrender of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-karak">Kerak</a> and Montréal; the garrisons refused, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> later freed him without ransom. After Sybilla's death in 1190 the barons, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balian-of-ibelin">Balian of Ibelin</a>, wanted Isabella married to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conrad-of-montferrat">Conrad of Montferrat</a>. The Itinerarium Regis Ricardi describes Humphrey as gentle and with a bad stammer. Isabella's mother, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-komnene-queen-of-jerusalem">Maria Comnena</a>, swore that the girl had been forced to marry at eight, and the papal legate Ubaldo Lanfranchi and Philip of Dreux annulled the childless marriage over the couple's protests.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Humphrey did homage to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england">Richard I of England</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limassol">Limassol</a> in May 1191, and being fluent in Arabic he opened talks for Richard with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>'s brother, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-adil">Al-Adil</a>, at Lydda. Some contemporaries accused him of hiring the men who murdered Conrad at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> on 28 April 1192, but modern historians hold him innocent. He died in 1198. Toron returned to the kingdom in 1229 under the treaty between Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-kamil">Al-Kamil</a> and Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Frederick II</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Humphrey IV of Toron” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His grandson <b>Humphrey IV</b> married Isabella, heir to the throne, but refused to be made king in 1186 and later lost her when barons forced their divorce.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-toron">Lordship of Toron</a></li></ul></section>
